By Vera Anyagafu
A new digital stamp at Europe’s airports is free. The €20 travel authorisation some Nigerians are talking about does not apply to Nigerian visa applicants to Europe.&nbsp;
Yet from Instagram vendors in Surulere to travel plazas in Onitsha, agents are charging Nigerian travellers up to N250,000 for both.
Since 10 April 2026, every Nigerian arriving in Paris or Frankfurt on a short-stay Schengen visa no longer gets an ink stamp.&nbsp;
A camera and fingerprint scanner logs you into Europe’s Entry/Exit System (EES) automatically, at no cost.
The other system, the European Travel Information and Authorisation System (ETIAS), is the source of the rumour.&nbsp;
It will eventually cost €20, valid for three years, but it is only for travellers who do not need a visa to enter Europe, like Americans, British, Australians and Canadians. For Nigerians, who still need a Schengen visa, ETIAS is never required. If someone in Nigeria is charging you for it, you are paying for something

